  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Factory audits fall into three groups: safety (PUWER guarding, LOTO isolation), quality (ISO 9001 layered process audits), and process or organisation (5S)</li><li>Shop-floor machinery safety is governed by the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 and PUWER 1998; lifting equipment falls under LOLER 1998</li><li>Most staff never fill in a dedicated app at the machine; they complete it later from memory, and the record suffers</li><li>Paying only for active users beats per-seat for a plant with a mix of floor and office staff, because dormant users are free</li><li>The software captures the audit and evidence; it does not make the machine safe or replace a competent PUWER inspection</li></ul></div><h2>What factory audit software is actually for</h2><p>Manufacturing sites run a lot of recurring checks: pre-use machine safety inspections, guarding audits, energy isolation before maintenance, layered process audits against ISO 9001, 5S walks, and near-miss and RIDDOR-reportable incident logging. Each has its own audience, from the HSE to a customer's supplier-quality team. What they share is that the record has to be accurate, timestamped and complete, and it has to survive scrutiny years later.</p><p>Software does not make any of these audits valid, and it does not make the machine safe. The guarding, the safe system of work and the competent person do. What software does is make the audit easier to complete correctly at the machine, harder to forget an item, and faster to deliver. A good tool means nobody reconstructs the audit from memory at the end of the shift.</p><h2>The three groups of factory audit</h2><h3>1. Safety audits</h3><p>Work equipment on a production line is governed by the Provision and Use of Work Equipment Regulations 1998 (PUWER), under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974. Machine guarding audits check fixed and interlocked guards, emergency stops, controls and safe access. Before maintenance, energy has to be isolated and locked off as part of a safe system of work, which is where lockout tagout and permit to work come in. Lifting equipment on site falls under LOLER 1998.</p><h3>2. Quality audits</h3><p>Layered process audits (LPA) check that a process is being run to standard, by different layers of management on a set frequency, and are a common way to keep an ISO 9001 quality management system honest between formal audits. The record ties each check to the standard, the auditor and the date.</p><h3>3. Process and organisation audits</h3><p>The 5S audit scores a work area on sort, set in order, shine, standardise and sustain, as part of a lean improvement programme. It is a scored, repeatable walk rather than a pass or fail inspection, and the trend over time matters as much as any single score.</p><h2>PUWER, LOTO and COSHH on the floor</h2><p>PUWER 1998 covers the provision and safe use of work equipment. Energy isolation before maintenance, and the lockout tagout that enforces it, are part of the safe system of work the employer must provide. Process chemicals bring in COSHH duties, and noise and vibration exposure carry their own regulations. Quickler can record that the correct isolation, permits and controls were in place and photograph the evidence, as part of the audit. It does not make the machine safe or assess exposure levels. Check the current regulations and your own risk assessments. This is not legal advice.</p><h2>What to look for in factory audit software</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to complete the audit at the machine, not afterwards</li><li><strong>Photo evidence at the point of observation:</strong> each finding tied to a timestamped image, not a folder sorted out that afternoon</li><li><strong>Live dashboard:</strong> the office should see a failed guard or a missed isolation in real time, not when someone emails the report</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing charges for office staff and contractors who never file. Quickler is £20 per active user per month, dormant users free, everything unlimited</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, auditor, machine or cell, every observation, for the HSE inspection, insurer or customer audit that surfaces years later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the audit and the evidence and gives the plant a live dashboard. It does not make the machine safe, that is the guarding and the safe system of work, and it does not replace a competent PUWER inspection by a qualified person where one is required. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your site.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is factory audit software?</h3><p>It is any tool that helps shop-floor staff record a safety, quality or process audit and produce a report: PUWER machine safety inspections, lockout tagout audits, layered process audits, 5S walks, near-miss logs. Options range from paper, to generic audit apps like iAuditor, to conversation-based tools like Quickler that run the workflow over WhatsApp so there is no app to install.</p><h3>Does a factory safety audit have to follow a set format?</h3><p>There is no single statutory report format for a machine safety audit. The legal duties come from the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 and PUWER 1998, and the audit has to capture the guarding, isolation and safe-system items a competent person and any HSE inspector would expect. Check the current regulations and your own risk assessments before relying on any template. This is not legal advice.</p><h3>Can I run factory audits over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflows run over the WhatsApp Business API. The auditor receives each question in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the completed report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Does the software make the machine safe or replace a PUWER inspection?</h3><p>No. Quickler records the audit and the evidence and gives the plant a live dashboard. It does not make the machine safe, that is the job of the guarding and the safe system of work, and it does not replace a competent PUWER inspection by a qualified person where one is required. It captures what the competent person found, with a timestamp and photo.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/machine-safety-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Machine safety inspection checklist (PUWER)</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/loto-lockout-tagout-audit-uk.html\">Lockout tagout (LOTO) audit</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/5s-workplace-audit-app-uk.html\">5S workplace audit app</a></li></ul>",
